  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Above normal temperatures and dry conditions \nwill prevail through Friday. \n\nOverall, warm and dry conditions will prevail through Friday. \nRidging aloft will steadily slip eastward and off the coast by \nFriday morning as a sharp trough shifts through the Upper \nMidwest and the Great Lakes region. At the surface, weak high \npressure will sit across the area today moving off the coast \nFriday as a strong cold front approaches from the northwest late\nin the day. We will again be near daily records today (see \nClimate section) followed by more mid to upper 80s for Friday. \n\nPatchy shallow ground fog is expected again this morning. While\nwidespread significant visibility reduction is not expected, \nthere could be a few patches that produce sharp visibility \nchanges over short distances. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Expect gusty winds and temperatures well below \nnormal this weekend behind a strong cold front.\n\nFriday: High pressure will gradually shift offshore on Friday \nas a strong cold front approaches from the northwest. This will \nyield warm conditions ahead of the boundary, with afternoon \nhighs in the mid to high 80s. Southwesterly winds are expected \nto increase somewhat during the day as the pressure gradient \ntightens ahead of the front. The latest model guidance generally\nfavors a relatively dry frontal passage with a few sprinkles \nlate Friday night into Saturday morning mainly across portions \nof northern Dorchester and Berkeley Counties. The front is \nforecast to cross the region overnight, with cooler and drier \nair filtering into the area from the north.\n\nSaturday: A noticeably cooler airmass should be present across \nthe region on Saturday in wake of the cold front. High \ntemperatures will struggle to reach around 70 degrees across \nmuch of the region, representing a substantial change from the \nwarmth experienced during the work week. Expect north-\nnortheasterly winds to become quite breezy with speeds 15-20 mph\nand gusts up to 25 mph. The combination of cooler temperatures \nand persistent winds will make conditions feel distinctly more \nlike fall. Saturday night will turn chilly as high pressure \ncontinues to build southward across the Carolinas and into \nGeorgia.\n\nSunday: Expect cool and breezy conditions to continue as high \npressure continues to expand into the Eastern CONUS. Afternoon \ntemperatures will range from low to mid 60s across much of the \nregion with upper 60s along the coastline. Overnight lows may \nfall into the upper 40s across inland locations, with readings \nin the 50s closer to the coastline. The combination of low \nhumidity, rain-free forecast, and periods of sunshine should \nproduce crisp, seasonable fall-like weather. \n\nThe center of surface high pressure should shift offshore of \nthe New England coast on Monday, resulting in winds to weaken \nand shift from the east-northeast. Temperatures across the CWA \nshould warm near seasonable normals, mid to upper 70s. Gradual \nwarming expected through mid-week.",
